00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:48.840
practices.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.810
Introduction-- what is
GCS and why it is awesome.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:57.400
So GCS, or Google Cloud
Storage, is GCP's object

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.730
or block storage product.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:01.810
It's a very simple product
to use, from the customer

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:04.519
perspective, because
of various reasons,

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:06.290
but two fundamental reasons.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:09.400
One is that GCS offers
a consistent API

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.380
across all different
storage classes.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.070
And the second is GCS offers
uniform online latency

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:18.050
characteristics across all
different storage classes.

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.440
So, whether you are interacting
with the hottest storage

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.110
class or the coldest
storage class,

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:25.930
your core doesn't need to
change because it pays the same.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.060
And your handling
in the code doesn't

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:30.520
need to change because across
all these different storage

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.620
classes you have the same
online latency characteristics.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.130
GCS is highly
reliable, GCS offers

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:41.320
various geo-redundancy
options, ranging from a region,

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.070
to a country, to a continent,
based on what your use case is.

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:47.800
And GCS is highly
cost effective.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.930
You can leverage object
lifecycle management

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:52.900
to spread your cost across
all different storage

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:54.680
classes of GCS.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.140
Now, last but not the least, and
the focus of the session, GCS

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:03.040
is highly secure and
is inbuilt with a bunch

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.260
of fundamental
security capabilities

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:10.180
that you get straight away
off the box when you use GCS.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.640
So in terms of key
features, I have already

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.830
mentioned about the
unified experience

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:17.680
through the APIs and the
latency characteristics.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.730
So GCS offers strongly
consistent listing worldwide

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:25.710
and what we see
is, other than GCS,

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.840
when other public cloud
storages are being used,

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.340
customers usually use
a key value database

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.830
to drive this consistency
because they do not

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:37.450
get it off the box with other
public cloud storage offerings.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:41.930
But that is an additional
unit of cost and management

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:44.520
which you do not
have to do with GCS.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.860
I've already mentioned
geo-redundancy.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.350
The other great aspect
of using GCS is you

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.600
do not need to worry
about scale at all.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.510
We have single customers in GCS
who scale to exabytes of data.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.170
Now, in terms of
the use cases, we

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:00.880
see a standard
storage class being

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:04.840
used in two different
classes of use cases.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:09.940
We see dual regional and
regional standard location

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.090
types being used for highly
compute intensive use cases,

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:16.510
for example, for high
performance computing,

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:19.690
or for analytics, or
for machine learning.

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:23.230
What we also see is
customers leverage

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:27.010
GCS's multi-regional
storage locations.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:30.100
[? CDM-like ?] characteristics
because of its intelligent edge

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:34.000
caching and routing capabilities
for content distribution use

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.470
cases.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:38.710
And nearline line great if
what you are looking to do

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:39.410
is backup.

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:43.000
Coldline is great if what you
are looking to do is archive.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.620
And the archive
storage class itself

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.390
is great if what
you are trying to do

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:51.130
is to do long term archival
for regulatory or compliance

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:52.160
purposes.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:55.330
So what we see is archive
storage class is a great tape

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:58.240
replacement, but once you have
your data in archive storage

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.150
class, your data is still
alive, it's in the serving tier.

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:05.890
And that brings me to the fact
that once you have your data

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:09.880
in GCS you have this great
advantage that the entire data

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:12.890
platform that is Google
is now open to you.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:14.887
So once you have
your data in GCS

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:16.970
whatever it is that you
want to do with your data,

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:20.170
whether you want to run
AI or machine learning

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.600
or you want to do
analytics, whatever

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.090
it is that you want to do you
can do once you have landed

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:27.100
your data in GCS.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:32.290
Now security is not an
afterthought for us in GCS.

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:37.090
We have built GCS with security
as a fundamental primitive,

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:39.620
and this starts with
Google's private network.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.210
So Google possibly has
the world's largest

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:44.750
private network.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.210
So we have thousands
of edge node locations,

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:49.440
we have hundreds of
POP node locations.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.580
So wherever you are
producing your data

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:55.420
or consuming your data from,
you are never too far away

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:59.800
from one of Google's
edge or POP locations.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.430
And how that helps
you a lot is, say

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:05.140
if you are in a
particular location

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.930
and you are writing your data
to a Google Cloud Storage

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:10.450
packet in a different region.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:13.210
With GCS, your
data packets enter

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:15.370
Google's closest
[? call, ?] and then

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.740
it traverses Google's
private network

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.790
all the way up to the region.

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:22.127
Now, if you compare it
with another public cloud

00:05:22.127 --> 00:05:23.710
vendor, what would
happen is your data

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:25.810
packets would pass through
the public internet

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.440
up to the region where
your data is stored.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.670
You have much higher
exposure that way.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:32.265
When you use GCS
your data packets

00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:34.630
are flowing through
Google's private network

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.350
for most of its lifetime.

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:40.950
So your exposure to public
internet is as low as possible.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:45.990
Encryption is another very
important aspect of security

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:49.890
and with GCS all your
data is always encrypted.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.720
So whether it is
your data in-flight,

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:56.670
or whether it is your data at
rest, all of your data in GCS

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:57.840
is always encrypted.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.670
And we offer you entire
range in terms of choice

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:04.998
for managing your
encryption keys.

00:06:04.998 --> 00:06:07.290
So whether you want to use
customer supplied encryption

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:09.780
keys, or do you want to use
customer managed encryption

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.970
keys, or hardware
security module,

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:15.690
or you just want to use Google's
default automatic encryption,

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:20.340
you have all your
choices when you use GCS.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:22.230
And then the last part
that I would emphasize

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:25.860
on GCS's fundamental--
security fundamentals

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.710
is, even if you use the
more sophisticated security

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:33.630
capability with GCS, for
example, if you use KMS keys,

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:38.550
or if you want to put your GCS
buckets behind the VPC security

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:42.570
firewall, you do not have
to pay any penalty in terms

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:44.700
of your performance or your SLS.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.850
We have built all the
security capabilities with GCS

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:51.030
from ground up so that your
experience of using them

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:53.730
is the same as if you
are using GCS, even

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:55.230
without these features.

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:57.130
In terms of the
performance and the SLS,

00:06:57.130 --> 00:07:00.030
there is absolutely no
penalty that you need to pay.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:01.590
With that, I'm going
to hand it over

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:04.200
to Chester, who's going to
take you through some security

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.005
features and some best
practices and then

00:07:07.005 --> 00:07:08.380
we're going to
end with the demo.

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:09.695
Take it over, Chester.

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:11.078
CHESTER CUN: Thanks, Sub.

00:07:11.078 --> 00:07:12.620
The features I'll
be going over today

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:15.920
are uniform bucket-level access,
IAM conditions, service account

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:19.970
HMAC keys, and V4 signatures.

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:22.250
Uniform bucket-level
access went GA last year,

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:23.810
and this is a
bucket-level setting

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.510
that lets you simplify access
control across all objects

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:27.980
within a bucket.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:30.620
When enabled permissions are
managed using the bucket's IAM

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:34.370
policy, disabling the use
of individual object ACLs.

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:37.070
Since all objects now share
the same configuration,

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:39.470
it's much easier to tell
if any objects are publicly

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:41.260
exposed to the internet.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.180
By inspecting your
bucket policy,

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:47.490
you can quickly determine who
can or cannot access your data.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.330
Uniform bucket-level
access also helps

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:51.280
you leverage a
security feature called

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:53.590
domain restricted
sharing, which enforces

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:55.900
that all members
of an IAM policy

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:57.700
must be within a
specified domain.

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:02.780
IAM conditions brings
attribute-based access control

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:05.780
to GCS, by extending
IAMs policy language

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:07.550
with conditional bindings.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:09.500
This recently GA
feature allows you

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:12.590
to grant access based on a
resource name or a request

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:13.760
timestamp.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:16.520
A simple example that
combines both attributes is

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:19.340
if you tried to grant future
access to all objects whose

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:22.110
names started with 2020 slash.

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:23.670
I've also included
a cell expression

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:25.740
at the bottom of the
slide that you could use

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.873
to implement such a condition.

00:08:27.873 --> 00:08:30.290
Some of you may already be
familiar with user account HMAC

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:33.309
keys, but I'd like to introduce
you to service account HMAC

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:36.039
keys, which are now GA.

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:38.980
Service account HMAC keys are
similar to user account in that

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:40.840
they're also a type
of credential that

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:42.679
can be used to
create signatures,

00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:45.527
which you can include
in your requests to GCS.

00:08:45.527 --> 00:08:47.110
These signatures
show that the request

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:49.430
is authorized by the
entity that owns the HMAC

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:53.590
key, delegating that entity's
access to the request.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:55.410
You manage service
account HMAC keys

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:59.290
by calling our APIs to generate
HMAC credentials, which consist

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:01.540
of an access key and a secret.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:04.060
So if you're already
using user account HMAC,

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:05.770
there's no need to
change your code.

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:07.900
You can simply update
the access key and secret

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:10.518
that your code is using.

00:09:10.518 --> 00:09:12.060
With service account
HMAC you'll also

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:14.340
get better key
lifecycle management.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:16.350
From the cloud console
UI, you can quickly

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:21.130
create, rotate, disable,
and delete your HMAC keys.

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:22.840
And lastly, service
account HMAC is

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:26.050
interoperable with other public
clouds, which will enable

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:27.110
your app for multi cloud.

00:09:29.834 --> 00:09:32.750
For customers who work with
multiple public clouds,

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:36.780
GCS has also gone GA with
our V4 signature support.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:39.120
V4 enables you to access
multiple object stores

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:41.010
with the same
client code and it's

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:44.592
available for both RSA and
service account HMAC keys.

00:09:44.592 --> 00:09:46.800
Now, I'd like to show you
how these features can help

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:49.120
you secure your data at scale.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:51.850
To start, I'll go over
fundamentals like org policies

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:54.520
and the differences between
Cloud IAM and GCS ACLs.

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:57.520
I'll also explain how uniform
bucket-level access simplifies

00:09:57.520 --> 00:09:58.952
access control at scale.

00:09:58.952 --> 00:10:00.910
And then I'll conclude
with some best practices

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:03.850
for using HMAC keys with GCS.

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:06.130
Within GCP, all
resources conform

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:07.710
to a resource hierarchy.

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:10.240
GCS is no different,
your buckets hold objects

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:12.010
and they're associated
with projects,

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:14.080
and projects can be
nested inside folders

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:16.550
and are tied to organizations.

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:18.020
Org policies are
settings that you

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:20.810
can figure at the org,
folder, or project level,

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:24.030
to enforce a service
specific behavior.

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:26.600
So remember that all resources
inherit from org policies set

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:29.120
higher in the hierarchy,
so if you set an org

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:31.820
policy at the org level,
it would cover all GCS

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:34.122
buckets in its tree.

00:10:34.122 --> 00:10:35.580
And lastly, org
policies themselves

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:37.680
are not retroactive
because they take effect

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:39.070
after they're enforced.

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:41.400
So hypothetically, if there
was an org policy that

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:44.400
prevented buckets from being
created in a specific region,

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.500
enabling it now wouldn't
impact any buckets

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:50.440
that were created before
that org policy was enforced.

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:53.730
The two org policies that we
recommend enabling with GCC

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:55.560
are domain restricted
sharing and

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.280
uniform bucket-level access.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:59.280
The first one prevents
users from adding members

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.580
outside your organization
to IAM policies controlling

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:04.405
access to your GCS resources.

00:11:04.405 --> 00:11:06.780
So if you tried to make the
contents of the bucket public

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:09.300
to the internet by adding all
users member to the bucket

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:11.880
policy, this org
policy, when enabled,

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:13.410
would block that operation.

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:16.050
And I'll cover this in the demo.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:18.750
The second org policy enforces
the use of uniform bucket level

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:20.820
access, so after
you enable this,

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:22.830
all newly created
buckets are required

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:26.640
to manage their access uniformly
through the bucket policy.

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:28.350
And this is also
covered in the demo.

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:29.730
The easiest way
to set org policy

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:31.405
is through the Cloud Console UI.

00:11:31.405 --> 00:11:33.780
For certain org policies, like
domain restricted sharing,

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:35.880
you will need to know
your organization's domain

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:37.420
ID, as well.

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:39.820
And if you don't have
it, totally fine.

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:42.360
You can always use
G Cloud to find it.

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:45.960
I'd like to go over a brief
history of GCS access control.

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:48.630
In the beginning, GCS
only supported ACLs,

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:51.780
and then Cloud IAM came along,
and GCS integrated with IAM

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.080
at the bucket level.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:55.178
So today, when you
configure access to GCS,

00:11:55.178 --> 00:11:56.970
you should be aware
that there are actually

00:11:56.970 --> 00:11:58.415
two systems at play here.

00:11:58.415 --> 00:12:01.790
There's Cloud IAM
and there's GCS ACLs.

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:04.200
Access can be granted
from either system,

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:07.010
but Cloud IAM behaves like
work policies in that they also

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:09.060
follow the resource hierarchy.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:12.160
So any object permissions, when
granted at the bucket level,

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:16.170
they automatically apply to all
objects underneath that bucket.

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:18.390
GCS ACLs on the other
hand, they do not

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:19.703
follow research hierarchy.

00:12:19.703 --> 00:12:21.120
They are used at
the object level,

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:24.540
and can only grant access
to individual objects.

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:28.060
In general, we recommend using
Cloud IAM to authorize access

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:31.650
to GCS because it is integrated
with so many security

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:33.870
and privacy features,
like domain restricted

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:36.290
sharing and Cloud [? analog. ?]

00:12:36.290 --> 00:12:38.510
Since objects can
have unique ACLs,

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:40.700
it gets harder to tell who
has access to your data

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:42.890
the longer your bucket
has been in production.

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:45.800
Imagine having to iterate across
millions or billions of objects

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:48.590
in order to check to see
if a specific user is

00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:51.445
granted or denied
access to any objects.

00:12:51.445 --> 00:12:52.820
You can see that
this can quickly

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:56.490
become a maintenance burden.

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:58.320
And our recommendation
for managing lots

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:01.710
of object ACLs at scale is
to not manage them at all.

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:03.750
By enabling uniform
bucket-level access,

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:06.090
you are disabling object
ACLs and using the bucket

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:08.420
policy for all access control.

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:10.310
Every object is
treated the same,

00:13:10.310 --> 00:13:11.810
and every object
benefits from all

00:13:11.810 --> 00:13:13.587
of IAM's feature integrations.

00:13:13.587 --> 00:13:15.170
An added benefit to
using this feature

00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:17.780
is that GCS can now share
access decisions for objects

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:19.208
within the same bucket.

00:13:19.208 --> 00:13:21.500
Caching is improved, which
is great for [INAUDIBLE] use

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:22.247
cases.

00:13:22.247 --> 00:13:24.080
And we understand that
some of our customers

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:27.020
continue to use GCS ACLs
for different reasons,

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:29.270
whether it's because of their
multi cloud architecture

00:13:29.270 --> 00:13:31.103
or because ACLs allow
you to share an object

00:13:31.103 --> 00:13:32.990
with an individual user.

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:34.580
In general it is
not a best practice

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:37.010
to put in end users
on your object ACLs,

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:39.360
so I'd like to recommend
some alternatives.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:41.450
First, signed URLs.

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:43.670
Signed URLs allow you to
delegate time limited access

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:45.170
to GCS resources.

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:48.530
Your code generates a URL which
contains authentication info

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:51.980
in the query string that's tied
to a specific identity, usually

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:53.420
a service account.

00:13:53.420 --> 00:13:56.930
And that URL is sent to
an end user for download.

00:13:56.930 --> 00:14:00.730
Second, if you notice that
certain groups of objects

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:03.720
happen to share the same access
pattern within your bucket,

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:06.790
like they have the
same object ACL set,

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:08.320
then this might
be a sign that you

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:12.190
should be using separate
buckets, one for each use case.

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:14.350
And lastly, if
your app makes use

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:16.600
of shared prefixes
in object naming,

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:19.390
then you could also use IAM
conditions to [? show ?] access

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:22.060
based on those prefixes.

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:25.240
And finally, I'd like to
explain some best practices

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:28.240
when using HMAC keys on GCS.

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:30.610
First off, we recommend
service account HMAC

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:32.283
over user account HMAC.

00:14:32.283 --> 00:14:33.700
Service accounts,
in general, they

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:35.530
offer more reliability
because they

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:37.840
are tied to the lifetime
of the cloud project,

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:39.500
whereas user accounts are not.

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.320
The user account can
easily be deleted

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:43.450
outside the context of GCP.

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:46.720
For example, when a collaborator
leaves the organization

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:49.870
or when an employee
leaves the company.

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:52.930
If this happens, deactivating
that user account would also

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:54.940
deactivate their
HMAC keys and this

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:57.370
would result in access denied
errors for any code that

00:14:57.370 --> 00:14:59.700
was relying on those keys.

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:01.970
So we definitely recommend
using service account HMAC

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:04.470
keys in a production service.

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:07.920
Other things we recommend, we
recommend rotating your keys

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:10.200
and only granting
your service accounts

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:13.230
just enough permissions
to accomplish whatever

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:17.140
task they're trying to do.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:20.340
And lastly, if you're
using V2 signatures,

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:22.980
we recommend setting
reasonable expiration times,

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:26.360
as well as eventually
migrating to V4.

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:28.400
V4 enforces a max
time limit of one

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:32.090
week, whereas V2 has no
limit which can be risky.

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:34.490
All right, time for a demo.

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:37.580
All right, let's begin the demo
by enabling the org policies

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:41.570
that I mentioned in the best
practices section before.

00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:45.510
To do this, we'll go to
the org policy editor,

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:49.020
and we will configure
domain restricted sharing

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:51.220
and uniform bucket level access.

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:54.360
So let's start
with the first one.

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:57.190
And for domain
restricted sharing,

00:15:57.190 --> 00:15:59.710
you're going to
need your domain ID,

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:01.770
and if you don't
know that off the top

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:03.160
of your head, no worries.

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:05.670
You can use G Cloud to
list the organizations

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:06.670
that you have access to.

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:11.700
You can see here, I have a test
org and this value on the right

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.320
is going to be my domain ID.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:17.160
So when I throw it here-- that
here into the policy value,

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:19.098
select save.

00:16:19.098 --> 00:16:20.380
Great.

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:24.070
Now only members of this domain
ID are allowed on IAM policies

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:28.110
anywhere in my project.

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:30.390
Go back to this org
policy view, select

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:35.280
enforce, and we will customize
and set the enforcement on,

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:37.490
as well.

00:16:37.490 --> 00:16:40.520
So now that I have
configured This org policy,

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:42.890
for uniform bucket level
access, any new bucket

00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:45.470
will have to use
that feature which

00:16:45.470 --> 00:16:49.100
will greatly simplify access
control across objects

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:51.750
in buckets.

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:54.160
From here, let's go
to a storage browser

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:57.260
and see these org
policies in action.

00:16:57.260 --> 00:16:58.170
So we create bucket.

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:04.819
Let's call this
bucket gcs-next-onair.

00:17:07.869 --> 00:17:12.405
We'll use the defaults for data
location and storage class.

00:17:12.405 --> 00:17:14.030
And we can see here
that the org policy

00:17:14.030 --> 00:17:18.339
I enabled earlier enforces
uniform for access control.

00:17:20.849 --> 00:17:23.920
I'm going to create
the bucket now.

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:26.290
And let's make sure domain
restricted sharing is also

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:27.770
in effect, as well.

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:30.070
So to do that, we'll
go to permissions

00:17:30.070 --> 00:17:34.960
and we'll try to add something
that's outside the domain.

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:38.590
If I were to add all users,
which would grant access

00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:42.700
to everyone making the
bucket publicly exposed

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:48.190
on the internet, that would
violate the domain restricted

00:17:48.190 --> 00:17:52.700
sharing org policy, and so
I am expecting this to fail.

00:17:56.110 --> 00:17:59.530
And we see that the
policy update failed

00:17:59.530 --> 00:18:02.980
and it's because the org
policy was put in place.

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:04.690
So that's good.

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:09.020
If we can't add all users
as a member, who can we add?

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:12.420
Well, most of the time when
you run a production service,

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:15.140
you want to use
service accounts.

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:20.650
So prior to this demo, I
had created an object viewer

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:22.725
service account.

00:18:22.725 --> 00:18:24.850
It doesn't actually have
object viewer permissions,

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:26.770
but I just named it
object viewer, just

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:29.370
for the sake of simplicity.

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:34.550
So if I go back
to storage browser

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:36.770
and I update the
permissions here,

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:40.560
I should be able to add that
service account to my bucket.

00:18:46.748 --> 00:18:52.230
And when I give it the same
permission that I tried before,

00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:56.450
storage object viewer which
has read access to objects,

00:18:56.450 --> 00:18:59.510
and I'm going to select save.

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:03.170
And because this is configured
at the bucket level,

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:05.270
my service account is
going to have access

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:07.700
to all objects
within this bucket.

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:09.772
As you can see, I haven't
added any objects yet,

00:19:09.772 --> 00:19:11.480
so I'm going to go
ahead and do that now.

00:19:14.030 --> 00:19:21.970
Here I have some folders that
have some text files in them.

00:19:21.970 --> 00:19:28.820
Let me drag and drop
them here onto the UI.

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:32.780
All three folders have
different prefixes but they

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:35.000
all have the same
text files in them--

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:37.250
apples, bananas and cherries.

00:19:37.250 --> 00:19:41.180
So how do we confirm that our
service account actually has

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:43.290
access to all these objects?

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:46.820
Well, easy way to do it is
to use service account HMAC.

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:50.030
You go to settings,
interoperability, create key

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:52.292
for your service account.

00:19:52.292 --> 00:19:54.500
I already have a key set up
here but I don't remember

00:19:54.500 --> 00:19:56.570
the secret for it,
so I am just going

00:19:56.570 --> 00:19:59.150
to go ahead and make a new key.

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:01.810
Got over to
terminal, you can see

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:03.560
that I have [? this get ?]
object metadata

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:05.090
with HMAC key script.

00:20:05.090 --> 00:20:07.100
This script reads from
environment variables

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:08.773
that I have to
export, so I'm going

00:20:08.773 --> 00:20:09.940
to go ahead and do that now.

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:18.140
Exported the access key, and
we'll do the secret next.

00:20:21.032 --> 00:20:22.480
Great.

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:26.780
So now if I run the
script and I try

00:20:26.780 --> 00:20:31.520
to access my bucket
objects, I should

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:33.200
see that access is granted.

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:40.460
And you can see that we
have successfully touched

00:20:40.460 --> 00:20:43.090
the metadata for this object.

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:46.980
Let me try some of
the other files.

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:48.550
Looks like access
has been granted.

00:20:48.550 --> 00:20:50.850
Great.

00:20:50.850 --> 00:20:53.495
So if you go back
to the browser,

00:20:53.495 --> 00:20:54.870
I mentioned earlier
that I wanted

00:20:54.870 --> 00:20:57.930
to show you an example
of IAM conditions.

00:20:57.930 --> 00:21:00.900
So with IAM conditions you
could set an object prefix,

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:06.030
which would limit the access to
objects with a certain prefix

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:07.330
in the name.

00:21:07.330 --> 00:21:10.900
So instead of granting access
to every object in the bucket,

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:14.280
let's say I only wanted to
grant access to those with 2020

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:15.610
in the name.

00:21:15.610 --> 00:21:18.518
So to do that, go back to
the bucket permissions page,

00:21:18.518 --> 00:21:21.060
I don't need to add the member
because the service account is

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:24.970
already on the bucket policy,
all I have to do is edit it.

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:28.390
So from here, I can
add a condition.

00:21:28.390 --> 00:21:31.130
Let me call it 2020 prefix.

00:21:31.130 --> 00:21:34.580
And for condition type,
we'll do resource name.

00:21:34.580 --> 00:21:39.990
Starts with, and we have to
specify a resource name format.

00:21:39.990 --> 00:21:45.080
So, the format's different
for each GCP service,

00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:48.770
so this table right
here is really nice.

00:21:48.770 --> 00:21:51.320
And you can see that
Cloud Storage Objects

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:55.080
follows this format, so
I'm going to grab that,

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:56.600
copy it in here.

00:21:56.600 --> 00:21:59.447
For object name, it's
not an object name,

00:21:59.447 --> 00:22:00.530
it's going to be a prefix.

00:22:00.530 --> 00:22:05.570
I'm going to do 2020
slash, and for bucket name

00:22:05.570 --> 00:22:10.220
I'm going to put gcs-next-onair,
in the bucket, click save.

00:22:13.190 --> 00:22:15.400
Now that I have this
setup, I'm going

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:18.920
to check to make sure that
I have access only to 2020

00:22:18.920 --> 00:22:22.550
and not the other two.

00:22:22.550 --> 00:22:25.970
2018 should fail
now, access denied.

00:22:29.750 --> 00:22:32.990
2019 should also
fail, I'm going to try

00:22:32.990 --> 00:22:37.700
some other files, also denied.

00:22:37.700 --> 00:22:40.340
But if we make it 2020,
access should be granted.

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:51.870
Great.

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:56.430
And that's how you set up a
object prefix IAM condition.

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:59.280
And with that, that
concludes the demo.

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:00.760
Thank you very much.

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:02.510
SUBHASISH CHAKRABORTY:
Thank you, Chester.

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:04.520
To conclude, as you have
seen in this session,

00:23:04.520 --> 00:23:07.430
GCS offers [? plan ?]
scale, object or blob

00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:10.160
storage with a simple
and unified API,

00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:12.230
and online latency
characteristics.

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:15.530
And it addresses a whole
wide range of use cases

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:18.320
we see customers use this for.

00:23:18.320 --> 00:23:21.710
Security is built into GCS
by design and default, not as

00:23:21.710 --> 00:23:22.980
an afterthought.

00:23:22.980 --> 00:23:26.270
And as you saw from
the various GA features

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:28.550
that Chester just
talked about, we

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:32.000
are continuing to innovate
and invest very heavily

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:35.390
in the security capabilities
to address your requirements

00:23:35.390 --> 00:23:37.250
and concerns.

00:23:37.250 --> 00:23:41.120
With GCS you can confidently
store your data at scale

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:46.070
and trust us to keep your
data secure and safe.

00:23:46.070 --> 00:23:48.560
With that, I would
recommend if you haven't yet

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:52.880
tried some of these GA features,
or if you haven't tried some

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:56.330
of these secretive best
practices that we discovered,

00:23:56.330 --> 00:23:58.140
give it a shot and let us know.

00:23:58.140 --> 00:23:59.830
Thank you.

